    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The African cichlid Astatotilapia burtoni is an extensively used model organism for social behavior due to the males’ ability to rapidly change in social status. Upon achieving social dominance, males perform distinctive aggressive and courtship behaviors. As in any kind of behavior analysis, these behaviors can be recorded by observers for further study. However, no set standard currently exists for determining the length of the timeframe necessary for analyzing behavior of A. burtoni or of other behavioral models, which can make comparisons between studies of even the same species difficult. We analyzed data from males of this species, using the list of behaviors they performed and the time at which they were performed. We then sampled random minutes within logs in order to determine how much time is truly needed to accurately construct an individual’s behavioral repertoire. Furthermore, we calculated mean behavior performed and coefficient of variation of behaviors to determine how they influence the required time. We found that mean rate of a behavior positively correlates with time needed to score a behavior, while coefficient of variation of behavior has a negative correlation. By determining how different factors can influence the amount of time needed to observe an animal to adequately represent its behavior, we can improve the efficiency of behavioral analysis of A. burtoni and identify principles of behavior that may be used to study other species.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
